 ],
 "literal": "Neem branch breaking and, Kāñci song singing and, / having ghee hands-possessing-people white mustard fumigating and, / all houses too making a bustling sound they indeed [became]. / Having burned and raged, will he strike? / From afar came indeed, the great noble man's chariot indeed.",
 "translation": "With neem branches being broken, and the Kāñci song being sung, and people with ghee-smeared hands fumigating white mustard, all the houses have become bustling with noise. Will he strike again, burning with rage? The chariot of the great noble warrior has arrived from afar.",
 "modern": "வேப்பங்கிளைகளை ஒடித்தும், காஞ்சிப் பாடல்களை பாடியும், கைகளில் நெய்யை ஏந்தியவர்கள் வெண்கடுகைப் புகைத்தும் கொண்டிருப்பதால், எல்லா வீடுகளும் ஆரவார ஒலியுடன் விளங்குகின்றன. சினந்து வெந்து பகைவரை அவர் மீண்டும் தாக்குவாரோ? பெருந்தகையாளனின் தேர் நெடுந்தொலைவிலிருந்து வந்துவிட்டது.",
 "explanation": "The poet Veḷḷai Māḷar describes the anxious atmosphere in a warrior's town after he returns severely wounded from battle. To protect him from evil spirits attracted to his wounds, the townspeople perform protective rituals: they break neem branches, sing the Kāñci song, and burn white mustard mixed with ghee. All the houses in the settlement are bustling with the noise of these protective activities. Despite his critical condition, the poet wonders if the warrior, burning with rage, will rise again to strike his enemies. The arrival of his chariot from afar highlights his unyielding heroic spirit, fitting the Vākai landscape.",
